<h2>How the dispute arose</h2>
<p>The appellant held a money decree of February 2019 for Rs 3,95,450 with interest, and filed execution in April 2019. The judgment debtor died during the execution, and proceedings continued against his widow as legal representative. On the decree holder&rsquo;s affidavit that the widow &ldquo;still holds&rdquo; a DDA flat in Janakpuri, the executing court attached the flat in April 2026 and issued a prohibitory order. The judgment debtor&rsquo;s sister then objected under Order XXI Rule 58 CPC: the flat was hers under their father&rsquo;s registered Will of 7 August 2007. The executing court sustained the objection and vacated the attachment; the decree holder appealed.</p>

<h2>The two statutory gates of execution</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Section 50 CPC</h3>
    <p>A decree against a deceased judgment debtor&rsquo;s legal representative is enforceable only against property of the deceased that came to the representative&rsquo;s hands.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">+</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Section 60(1) CPC</h3>
    <p>Only saleable property — or property over which the judgment debtor has a disposing power for his own benefit — can be attached and sold.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Justice Harish Vaidyanathan Shankar heard the appeal at the admission stage with consent, since everything turned on the construction of the Will rather than on disputed facts. Reading the Will as a whole — the approach commanded by Sections 82 and 87 of the Indian Succession Act — the Court found that the father had bequeathed the flat to the daughter, while giving the son a right to construct a second floor and reside on it. No right of sale accompanied that right. The appellant&rsquo;s reliance on Section 88 of the Succession Act — under which the later of two irreconcilable clauses prevails — failed because the clauses were reconcilable; there was no conflict to resolve in his favour.</p>

<h2>A personal right is not attachable property</h2>
<p>The consequence followed from Section 6(d) of the Transfer of Property Act: an interest in property restricted in its enjoyment to the owner personally cannot be transferred, and what cannot be transferred is not &ldquo;saleable property&rdquo; within Section 60(1) CPC. The Court drew on the Supreme Court&rsquo;s decision in <em>Kaivelikkal Ambunhi</em>, the Full Bench in <em>Maj. Gen. Rajinder Singh Chowdhary</em> and a Division Bench in <em>Shakuntla v. Rajinder Singh Deswal</em>, and distinguished the absolute-grant line of cases — where a restraint on an absolute estate is void — because here no absolute estate was ever granted. There was a further, simpler point: the Will itself recorded that the second floor had never been built, and the decree holder did not rebut the objector&rsquo;s averment to that effect. A right to build a floor that was never built left nothing in the judgment debtor&rsquo;s hands to attach.</p>

<h2>Lessons for decree holders and objectors</h2>
<p>For decree holders, the decision is a reminder that execution against heirs begins with tracing what the deceased actually owned — a step that an affidavit asserting that the widow &ldquo;still holds&rdquo; a flat cannot replace. Title documents, mutation records and, where a Will exists, its actual dispositions determine what Section 50 makes available. For family members facing attachment of property they claim as their own, Order XXI Rule 58 is the designed remedy: it is adjudicated like a suit, and an order on it is appealable as a decree, as this very appeal demonstrates. The fifteen-day delay in refiling was condoned without difficulty — but the appeal itself failed on the law.</p>
<div class="note">The Court left the decree holder at liberty to pursue other assets of the judgment debtor and of the estate. Execution strategy — which assets to attach, in what order, and how to meet third-party objections — is case-specific, and this article is informational only.</div>
